  "title": "The good news",
  "subtitle": "how a person is made right with God",
  "concepts": [
   "Who God is",
   "What went wrong",
   "What Jesus did",
   "How to respond"
  ],
  "motto": [
   "A father running.",
   "A door left open."
  ],
  "lead": [
   "This study is for you if you are not a Christian, or are not sure what Christians actually believe. It reads a few passages to answer one question: how can a person be put right with God? It teaches one habit for reading them: before you ask what you must do, ask what God has done."
  ],
  "opening": [
   {
    "k": "quote",
    "r": "JHN.3.16",
    "label": "John 3:16"
   }
  ],
  "frame": [
   "The word ",
   {
    "i": "gospel"
   },
   ", which Christians use often, means “good news”. News is a report of something that has already happened. Advice tells you what to do. The Bible does give commands, but the message at its centre is news: God has acted, in history, through Jesus. So as you read, look first for what God has done. Ask what you should do only after that. The passages below are written in this order, and so is this study."
  ],
  "selection": [
   "This is a selection, not a census. The Bible says far more about God, sin and salvation than one page can hold. This study walks two passages fully. The first is three stories Jesus told in Luke 15. The second is the first public sermon about Jesus after His death, in Acts 2. It quotes a few other verses along the way and links to the studies that read them fully. Six questions readers often ask are answered at the end."
  ],
  "close": {
   "title": "Ask what God has done",
   "way": [
    "Here is the habit to carry to the next passage. Read the gospel as news before you read it as advice. Ask first what God has done: in the shepherd who searches, the father who runs, the Jesus God raised. Then ask what it calls for. Turn, trust, and come in."
   ],
   "invite": [
    "If you have not yet trusted Christ, none of this begins with you getting yourself in order. It begins with Him. The father in Luke 15 ran before his son finished speaking. Turn from your sin and trust Jesus as Lord, because “There is no salvation apart from personal faith in Jesus Christ as Lord”. Tell Him so, in your own words. Then find a church, tell someone there, and ask to be baptized."
   ],
   "question": [
    "Which son are you, the one far away or the one standing outside, and what would it take for you to go in?"
   ],
   "again": [
    "Read the passage again before you answer."
   ],
   "passage": "LUK.15.11-32"
  }
